|:--|:--|:--|--:|
| \`uploads.example.com\` | \`PUT\` ‹path hidden› | path not allowed | 1 |
| \`uploads.example.com\` | \`INVALID\` ‹path hidden› | method not allowed on this path | 1 |

<sub>The path of a refused request is never shown: whoever sent it chose it, and it can carry data. If the job needs one of these requests, run it in analyze mode without that host's \`http-filter\` entry: the suggested \`http-filter\` then covers it. Review the rule before you add it: a write is how data leaves.</sub>

<details open>
<summary>🧰 <b>Suggested <code>http-filter</code></b> — collapsed 12 requests → 2 rules (level B); 1 write endpoint kept specific</summary>

<sub>Advisory, built from this job's intercepted traffic — never applied for you. Reads collapse to one \`"*"\` line per host; write paths keep their structure, with ids and hashes as \`*\`; git push and fetch stay pinned to the repos this job used. GitHub platform hosts are left out (the built-in platform allowlist covers them). Add each host to \`allowed-domains\` too: a filter never grants access.</sub>

\`\`\`yaml
